Z||000005||JohnnyK||04-23-2001||08:50 PM||||If it cranks over, your battery is hooked up fine. SOUNDS like you have no spark, if you did change the distributor and rotor, but you say you checked that. Did you take the ACTUAL distributor out or move it around? If you did, you will need to find TDC of cyl#1 and reset firing order. If you didn't, and thats not the problem, take out the plugs after trying to start it and see if there is fuel on the plugs. BUT, if it started before, and it won't now after you replaced the distributor and rotor, the problem is probably in the spark/ignition. It just hit me! You probably mixed up the wires when reattaching to the distributor. Please tell me you took the old one and new one, and moved them one by one to the right position??||||reg||1
Z||000006||anthony_86gt||04-23-2001||08:55 PM||||No i didnt change them one by one, but the wires are on right.  cause i thought that was the problem until i got a picture of the firing order.  can you send me something to check the order again.  tomorrow ill check again but im for sure its fine.  i have spark for sure too.  any other ideas?||||reg||1
Z||000007||MooCow||04-23-2001||10:32 PM||||Check your coil.  Also check your coil-up in the distributor.  Mine was the plug that goes from the ignition modual to the coil was not connected all the way.  Also bring the igniton modual to Autozone to have them test to see if it is good or not.<BR>I had the same problem.  <BR>Where at in MI is Monroe??<P>                                 Thanks Ken<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig.jpg"> <BR>87 GT, 3.4 engine with a 4:10 tranny, and Viper Yellow!||||reg||1
Z||000008||fyerstarter||04-23-2001||10:42 PM||||Check for spark. Pull a plug wire, stick a screwdriver or other metal object in there, ground it to the chassis and have a friend crank it over. If you see spark you have spark. You can also use a plug for this if you want to pull it. Also make sure you are not touching it when you turn it over  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> You say you smell fuel so prob not a fuel problem. You can check tho by opening the shrader valve under the intake plenum on the right side and see if you have fuel pressure. If you have fuel and spark then you prob have your wires on wrong or maybe they are crossed.<P>Does it chug and try to start or just nothing at all?<P><p>[This message has been edited by fyerstarter (edited 04-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||anthony_86gt||04-24-2001||02:59 PM||||i have spark.  moocow, what do you mean by the coil.  the one on the distributor?  can you give me some more detail.  if i have spark wouldnt the coil be working???  thanks for these ideas but nothin yet.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Spektyr||04-24-2001||03:21 PM||||First off, you have to be sure you're getting a hot blue spark.  You can have a weak spark that won't fire the cylinders (but I'd think it'd at least chug a bit, I could be wrong).<P>As for the firing order... if you're standing at the back bumper looking forward into the engine compartment:<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>the number one cylinder is all the way to the right, closest to you (near the dogbone).<BR><LI>Number 2 is on the opposite side on the right, directly opposite number one.<BR><LI>3 is the middle one, close to you, next to number 1.<BR><LI>4 is on the far side middle opposite number 3<BR><LI>5 is near side all the way to the left.<BR><LI>6 is far side all the way to the left.  <BR></UL><P>The little drawing in Haynes shows the number one terminal on the distributer as being towards the intake, slightly away from where you're standing.  In my car it's near the intake, but close to where you're standing.<P>My distributer has a 'flat side'.  If the flat side is at the bottom, viewed from the top, the top-most post would be number 6, and the first one clockwise would be number 1.<P>Hope that helps (and hope I'm right).<P>If you haven't turned the distributor, it should be timed just like it was before.  If you have turned it a little, you should at least be able to get it running so you can time it.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000011||Spektyr||04-24-2001||03:27 PM||||Just thought of another way to illustrate it.<P>ENGINE:<P>REAR WINDOW<P>6    4    2<P>5    3    1<P>REAR BUMPER<P><BR>(MY) DISTRUBUTOR:<P>.....6...<BR>..5.....1<BR>..........<BR>..4.....2<BR>.....3...<BR>-------<BR>(flat side)<BR>the periods are used to make the numbers hold posistion... they all end up on the left otherwise.<P>At least that's the way my distributer is wired, and mine runs.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?<p>[This message has been edited by Spektyr (edited 04-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Mach10||04-24-2001||05:31 PM||||Sounds like you are flooded out right now. Let it sit for a few extra hours. While doing that, take the distributer apart, then reassemble. Been there, done that.||||reg||1
